[HYP] Unauthenticated id-decode reach into a 51-field user object, now reachable at two independent endpoint paths
class: IDOR
asset: graphql-api.app.cineplex.de — queryType userById(id) / order(id) / ticket(id) / userByQr(qrCode), chaining through Order.user and Ticket.user, at both `/` and `/graphql` (staging identical)
confidence: 82
reasoning: Demonstrated live this cycle, twice, on a path never tested before. On `/graphql` an unauthenticated `userById(id:"0")` returns 200/744 B `INVALID_ID` naming `decodePublicId`, while `currentUser` on the identical request shape returns 200/836 B `UNAUTHENTICATED` — byte-for-byte the same pair previously observed on `/`, so the auth-omission is a property of the resolver, not of one route. Auth is demonstrably enforced on the same schema, at the same path, in the same second, so reaching a decode error unauthenticated is an observed fact and not an inference. The returned type is `User` = 51 fields including full identity, `onlineTicketingToken` (bearer credential for the separate ticketing system), `inviteCode`, `linkedAccounts`, `blocked`/`blockedReason`/`blockedText`, complete financial history, and `privileges:UserPrivileges!` with NON_NULL `rootRole`/`adminRole`/`supportRole`. `order(id) → user` and `ticket(id) → order → user` reach the identical object without `userById` ever being called, and `order.pickupCode:Int` / `ticket.qrCode` are redemption artifacts on those same paths. Not demonstrated: whether the branch after a *valid* decode enforces ownership.
evidence_needed: ONE consented account, its own publicId. With it, call `userById(<own-id>)` with no `Authorization` header at all. Data returned means the resolver is reachable pre-auth for a valid id and the finding is unauthenticated PII plus `onlineTicketingToken`/`inviteCode` disclosure, with nothing left to prove. `UNAUTHENTICATED` means the gate is present and only the post-decode ownership check remains, which then needs a second consented account. Independent second question, higher value because it needs no user id: does `order(id)` return `order.user` for an order that is not the caller's.
verify_steps: Passive half complete and re-verified this cycle; reproduction is one unauthenticated GET: `GET /graphql?query={userById(id:"0"){id}}` against `https://graphql-api.app.cineplex.de/graphql` → expect 200, `INVALID_ID`, 744 B, with `x-powered-by: Express`. The exploit half is a POST carrying the account's own id and must not be run unauthenticated or against any id not consented to. Do not request a second party's id and do not read any returned field value — the goal is gate behaviour, not data. Repeat the same test on `/graphql` as well as `/`, since a fix scoped to one path leaves the other open.
impact: If the post-decode ownership check is absent: unauthenticated or cross-tenant disclosure of complete customer identity, a live bearer token for the ticketing system, full purchase and invoice history, the privileged-staff population, plus ticket and order redemption artifacts — reachable through at least three resolvers across two endpoint paths. Critical, and the finding most likely to be accepted, because the pre-auth reach is demonstrated and the impact is enumerated rather than asserted. If the gate is present, this degrades to a documented attack surface with no demonstrated flaw, and I will report it that way.
