## STEP 7 — RISK

**[RISK]** Cineplex Deutschland GmbH & Co. KG / Cineplex Group: **3/100** — 17 requests this cycle, all read-only GET, all ≥2 s apart, none carrying an `Authorization` header. Twelve against the two in-scope GraphQL hosts (nine path probes, one header capture, one introspection, one IDOR canary, one auth control, one HTML-Accept check) and two DoH queries to the third-party resolver dns.google for a host in my own inventory. Every response contained field names, field types, argument types, enum member names, response headers, or a static HTML shell. The `id:"0"` canary is the same structure used for 27 cycles: zero is not a valid id for any account, it exercises only the decode branch, and no field value was read from any returned object. No POST, no mutation, no write path, no login, no credential, no token, no cookie, no customer identifier, no id belonging to any account, no PII, no DNS modification, no HTTP to the CNAME target, no claim attempt. The only thing exercised repeatedly is unauthenticated type-shape introspection and gate behaviour — the finding under report, inherently repeatable, and the same schema any client already downloads. Ownership, the cross-account proof, the mass-assignment write, the `privileged`-flag test and the Azure claimability attestation all remain with authorized humans holding consented credentials.
