Complete picture. Writing up the cycle.

## STEP 1 — DELTA

**[NEW]** `graphql-api.app.cineplex.de` — the CORS layer throws instead of denying, producing an unauthenticated remote 500 with a server stack trace. Any request carrying a non-allowlisted `Origin` on any path returns `500 text/html`/1004 B whose body is `Error: Not allowed by CORS` plus a 10-frame Node stack trace. The mechanism is the `cors` npm package's `origin` **callback** raising an exception rather than returning `callback(null, false)`, surfacing through Express's default error handler. Attribution is solid, not edge-generated: the 500 carries `x-powered-by: Express` and a body containing `/var/task/graphql.js`, `/var/task/node_modules/cors/lib/index.js`, and express router internals — Cloudflare does not synthesize those. Bodies are **byte-identical** for `https://attacker.example` and `https://second-origin.test` (md5 `eb8ef815…`), so rejection is value-independent. No valid query, credential, or token is required to trigger it.

**[NEW]** CORS allowlist is **real and enforcing**, which kills my declared escalation path. Disallowed: `attacker.example`, `second-origin.test` → 500. Allowed: `null`, `https://cineplex.de`, `https://app.cineplex.de` → 200. `vary: Origin` appears only on the no-Origin response, confirming a CORS-aware layer is deliberately in play. Preflight `OPTIONS` returns `403`/42 B with **no `access-control-*` header for either allowed or disallowed origin**. There is no origin reflection, no wildcard, and no `Allow-Credentials: true` anywhere.

**[NEW]** Correction that matters more than the 500: **CORS is not a mitigating control for H1 or H2, and must never be reported as one.** CORS is browser-enforced only — it does not constrain curl, a mobile app, or any server-to-server client, and here the preflight 403s even for first-party origins, so the allowlist is doing no protective work at all against non-browser callers. Anyone reading the IDOR report needs this stated, or they will credit a control that provides none.

**[CHANGED]** `probe-results.md` gains no usable CORS evidence — the harness cannot set `Origin` or issue `OPTIONS` through its path-based probe format, so its silence is not counter-evidence. No conclusion here is drawn from it.

