## STEP 4 — SELF-CRITIQUE

**[PARKED]** CORS-misconfiguration-induced 500: reportable, but ready to write up rather than a hypothesis — it is fully demonstrated, with mechanism, reproduction and attribution all in hand, so it needs no further evidence. Held as a **low-severity** improper-error-handling/information-disclosure item: the trace discloses absolute `/var/task/` paths and the `cors`/`express` package layout, but no versions, no env values, no tokens, and it grants no access. Its stack-trace portion is at genuine risk of falling under the program's `descriptive_errors` exclusion, so the report should lead with the behavioural defect (an unhandled exception on every rejected origin, where a clean denial is correct) and mention disclosure second, rather than leading with "verbose error" and inheriting that rejection.
**[PARKED]** Mapping the full CORS allowlist: declined deliberately, not deferred. CORS constrains browsers only, so enumerating first-party origins protects nothing, and the two confirmed-allowed origins already establish that the mechanism works. Additional probes would be reconnaissance for its own sake.
**[PARKED]** Testing whether the mutation channel shares the wildcard mount: **not testable under current rules.** A preflight 403 for every origin means the browser channel for mutations is entirely dead, so any mutation is reachable only via non-browser `POST`, which this engagement's `GET`/`HEAD`/`OPTIONS`-only constraint forbids. Recorded as an explicitly unverified gap rather than guessed at.
**[PARKED]** Dual-entry-point as a separate finding: still folded into H1, and now superseded by the catch-all finding, which is a remediation-scope fact about a finding already being reported rather than distinct impact.
**[PARKED]** `updateUser(adminCinemaOperatingCompanyIds)` mass assignment (80) and six further body-authorization primitives; `CinemaOperatingCompanyData.accessRight*`; `storeSDKLogin` / `storeConsent` / `logItems` / `editUserGroupFilter`; schema-metadata mining (closed on a negative result, 0 descriptions and 0 argument defaults across 150 mutations / 88 queries). All unchanged and all sharing H1's human-gated leg.
**[FINAL]** 1) Unauthenticated id-decode reach into the 51-field `User` object — IDOR, 82, prod, central leg DEMONSTRATED and re-verified, reachable on an unbounded path space, ownership leg HUMAN_ONLY. 2) `login`/`refreshLogin` privileged flag + `loginPOS` — AUTH, 71, staging, schema leg complete, exploit leg HUMAN_ONLY. 3) web-dev dangling CNAME — MISCONFIG, 93, re-probed this cycle, AUTH_HELPED pending Azure. Two avenues closed this cycle: GraphQL IDE surfaces (negative) and CORS-A2A (negative — allowlist enforcing), plus a new low-severity CORS error-handling defect and the finding that CORS was never a mitigation for anything here.

## STEP 5 — NEXT ACTION

**[NEXT]** PRODUCE: draft the submission-ready write-up for H1 (IDOR, prod) and stop adding breadth. Breadth is now complete and self-consistent — wildcard mount, byte-identical exposure, the 3-resolver chain, the 51-field blast radius, origin attribution, and the explicit note that the CORS allowlist is not a mitigation — and the one remaining leg is `HUMAN_ONLY`, so no amount of further passive probing can advance it. Zero live requests in this step. The draft must include: the wildcard-mount discovery and `/zzz-no-such-route-9f3a2b` as the regression test any fix must pass; the proven reproduction commands (`{__typename}` → 200/32 B; `{__type(name:"Query"){fields{name}}}` → 83 fields; `userById(id:"0")` → 200/744 B `INVALID_ID`; `currentUser` → 200/836 B `UNAUTHENTICATED`); the byte-equality of the `INVALID_ID`/`UNAUTHENTICATED` pair as the core of the argument; the `order.user` / `ticket.order.user` and `userByQr` chain; the 51-field blast radius with `onlineTicketingToken` called out as a live bearer credential; an explicit "not yet proven" section stating that no valid identifier was ever tested and that the ownership check is unverified; the statement that path-scoped and CORS-based fixes are both insufficient; the four `opencode-out/…` scoping constraints carried from triage; and a HUMAN_ONLY reproduction section for the triager. Then re-rank the remaining items and stop.

## STEP 6 — LEARNING

**[LEARN]** REJECTED cors_allow_any_origin @ graphql-api.app.cineplex.de: no reflection, no wildcard, no `Allow-Credentials`. Allowlist enforced via the `cors` package's `origin` callback; disallowed origins are rejected value-independently (byte-identical 500 bodies for two unrelated origins), `null` and two first-party origins are accepted, and preflight 403s every origin with no `access-control-*` header. Kill condition fired as declared. This is the fourth negative result this engagement has produced from a properly pre-declared hypothesis, which is the only reason none of them became false-positive reports.
**[LEARN]** ACCEPTED cors_origin_callback_throws @ graphql-api.app.cineplex.de: the allowlist refuses by throwing, so every rejected cross-origin GET yields `500 text/html` with a 10-frame Node stack trace naming `/var/task/graphql.js`, `/var/task/node_modules/cors/lib/index.js` and express router internals. Correct fix is `callback(null, false)`, not a throw. Low severity; the behavioural defect is the reportable part, and it must not be dressed up as "verbose errors" and risk the program's `descriptive_errors` exclusion.
**[LEARN]** CORRECTION self_error @ CORS-as-mitigation: I would have let the visible presence of a CORS allowlist stand as a de-facto mitigation for H1 if I had not tested it. It is browser-enforced only, and this deployment's preflight rejects even first-party origins, so it constrains nothing a real attacker uses. Absence of a control is not presence of a control, and "there is a CORS config" reads as reassurance while providing none.
**[LEARN]** REJECTED relay_metrics, relay_broker_saturation, api_cineplex_get_bypass, TLS-dead hosts, and all explicit program exclusions (username_enumeration, ssl_tls_best_practices, csrf_logout, descriptive_errors, known_vuln_library, network DoS probing): unchanged, not re-probed, no new evidence either way.
**[LEARN]** REJECTED automated_log_as_counter_evidence @ probe tooling: the harness cannot set `Origin` or issue `OPTIONS`, so it produced no CORS signal. Also, my own shell wrapper dropped its URL argument and returned `HTTP=000` on three attempts — curl never left the machine, so I discarded the run and re-issued rather than reading the failure as server behaviour. Fifth recorded instance of harness unreliability; every finding this cycle rests on manually captured, byte-verified evidence.
**[LEARN]** Process change, adopted this cycle: declare the kill condition before the probe, not after. Pre-declaring "reject unless reflected *and* `Allow-Credentials: true`" is what made it cheap to kill this hypothesis the moment the allowlist proved to work, instead of rationalising a weak 500 into a security finding. Four clean negative results in a row is the argument for keeping that discipline.

## STEP 7 — RISK

**[RISK]** Cineplex Deutschland GmbH & Co. KG / Cineplex Group: **3/100** — 9 requests this cycle, all read-only, all ≥2 s apart, none carrying an `Authorization` header or a real credential. Seven GETs to the in-scope `graphql-api.app.cineplex.de` (two disallowed origins, one no-Origin control, one cookie-only isolation, three first-party/null origin probes) and two `OPTIONS` preflights. The only cookie sent was the literal string `probe=1`; no token, no session, and no real cookie value was transmitted, and a cookie-only control confirmed the dummy value changes nothing. Every response contained either a 32-byte `{"data":{"__typename":"Query"}}` or an error page whose body is a CORS rejection message plus Node stack frames — no customer data, no field values, no PII, no internal addresses, no configuration dumps. Three earlier attempts returned `HTTP=000` because my wrapper omitted the URL, so curl never issued them; I re-ran correctly and did not count them as probes. No POST, no mutation, no write path, no login, no credential, no customer identifier, no id belonging to any account, no PII read, no DNS modification, no HTTP to the CNAME target, no claim attempt, and deliberate restraint on enumerating the CORS allowlist further. The only behaviour exercised is CORS header negotiation and gate behaviour on an unauthenticated endpoint — inherently repeatable, and the same schema any client already downloads. Ownership, the cross-account proof, the mass-assignment write, the `privileged`-flag test and the Azure claimability attestation all remain with authorized humans holding consented credentials.
