- [FINAL] 1) Staging env-gate-absent unauthenticated `PASSWORD_RESET` oracle + internal LOGIN topology leak, AUTH, **85**, staging, authorization leg DEMONSTRATED, code-retrieval leg HUMAN_ONLY — **file as a severity upgrade 6.5→8.1 on the existing staging report.** 2) Pre-auth id-decode into the 51-field `User` object across 4 resolvers + 2 chaining paths on an uncappable path set, IDOR, **84**, prod, central leg DEMONSTRATED, ownership leg HUMAN_ONLY. 3) `web-dev` dangling CNAME, MISCONFIG, **93**, AUTH_HELPED. Supporting, bundled into #2: unauthenticated service-health oracle and anti-piracy config disclosure (CWE-200, 3.1), plus `testing_forceDeleteUser` present in the prod schema. Carried, not re-read: `login(privileged)`/`loginPOS`, AUTH, 71, HUMAN_ONLY.

**STEP 5 — NEXT ACTION**

[NEXT] PROBE: sweep the 34 canaryable required-argument Query fields with obviously-synthetic scalar dummies, because that is the one remaining unswept dimension and it is precisely where the 4 pre-auth id decoders and both `testing_*` fields were found. Unauthenticated GET, browser UA, `Accept: application/json`, ≥2 s apart, on `https://graphql-api.app.cineplex.de/api/graphql`, one field per request, `{field(<dummy>){__typename}}` only. Prioritise the identity- and value-bearing ones: `voucherInstanceByQR(qrCode:"0")`, `voucherInstancesByOrderNumber(orderNumber:"0")`, `checkVoucherCode(voucherCode:"0")`, `userByQr(qrCode:"0")`, `beaconById(id:"0")`, `notification(id:"0")`, `targetGroup(id:"0")`, `getImdbIds(key:"0")`, `reverseGeocode(latitude:0,longitude:0)`. Record status, byte length, and `extensions.code`. Stop immediately on any field that RESOLVES or returns an `extensions.url`, and do not use any real voucher, ticket, or order number. Skip `testing_forceDeleteUser` outright.

**STEP 6 — LEARNING**

- [LEARN] ACCEPTED staging_env_gate_bypass @ `graphql-api.app.staging.cineplex.de`: the highest-severity finding of the session. The lesson is narrower than "staging is weak": **prod and staging implement the same field with different authorization, and the difference is a single missing gate, not a schema difference.** Introspection cannot show this — both environments expose an identical, non-deprecated `PASSWORD_RESET` code-minting field. Only executing the same query in both environments and comparing the outcome reveals it. Schema parity is not security parity, and the direction of the difference is the finding.
- [LEARN] ACCEPTED internal_service_topology_disclosure @ same host: a 405 rather than a 404 from a disclosed internal path is the difference between a route that does not exist and one that exists and rejected my method. Reading status codes instead of only error text is what turned an error message into a live-endpoint claim. And the leak is isolated to the `testing_*` resolvers, which I only know because I tested two unrelated resolvers and got clean errors back — the negative control is what kept the finding from being overstated as host-wide.
- [LEARN] CORRECTION self_error @ judgment: `testing_forceDeleteUser` is live on the **prod** Query root, which contradicts the earlier name-only "10 staging-only account mutations" note. Prod is protected by a resolver-level environment gate, not by schema absence — a distinction that only matters once you find the field is in the prod schema, and it also means the prod protection is one `if` statement away from failure. I did not test it, and the correct instinct is to record presence and hand the behavioral question to a human.
- [LEARN] ACCEPTED preauth_config_disclosure @ both envs: enumerating the 39 no-arg and all-optional Query fields took 39 requests and produced two new findings. Two of the three live fields are configuration rather than data, and one of them hands over the exact DSP parameters of an anti-piracy control. Data endpoints being gated while config endpoints are not is a common and very cheap thing to miss, because config endpoints return small boring objects that look like noise next to the 51-field `User` type I had been fixated on.
- [LEARN] CORRECTION self_error @ method: **fourth instance of one class.** I wrote "host closed, avenue parked" and then produced three new unauthenticated objects in the same cycle. The bound I had proved was on *path* space, and I let it stand as a bound on the *host*. What actually happened is that I had never enumerated the *field* space at all. Every finding this cycle came from enumerating a dimension I had assumed was covered. The cost of this class is not a wrong line, it is a self-certification that stops probing.
- [LEARN] REJECTED authless_id_guessing @ `graphql-api.app.cineplex.de`: 4/4 decoders reject synthetic and well-formed ids with type-named `INVALID_ID` and no existence oracle; `null` refused at `ID!`. Pre-auth reach is real, guessability is not. Protective, and it constrains how the IDOR may be described in the report.
- [LEARN] REJECTED graphql_ide_exposure, secondary_rest_surface, systemic_error_leak: all negative on evidence this cycle, each closed with a named count rather than an assumption.

**STEP 7 — RISK**

[RISK] Cineplex Deutschland GmbH & Co. KG / Cineplex Group: **2/100** — 47 requests this cycle, all GET, all read-only, all ≥2 s apart, none carrying an `Authorization` header. The `testing_getConfirmationCode` probes used a reserved RFC 2606 `.invalid` address (`poc-nonexistent-9f3a2b@example.invalid`) that cannot resolve or belong to any account, so no code was minted, no mail was sent, and no real identity was touched; I stopped at the 405 rather than changing method against the disclosed internal LOGIN endpoint, and I did not invoke `testing_forceDeleteUser` at all. All other probes selected `__typename` alone or a fixed set of non-PII scalars (sample rates, enum values, health text). No POST, no mutation, no write path, no login, no credential, no token, no customer identifier, no voucher/ticket/order number, no PII, no DNS modification, no claim attempt, no HTTP to the CNAME target. One `cinema(id:"0")` returned a bare HTTP 502; I recorded it as an observation and deliberately did not probe availability further, since DoS and adverse-impact testing are explicit program exclusions. The net safety result this cycle is that a destructive field was found and deliberately left alone, and an unauthenticated code-minting field was characterized without minting anything.
