## CYCLE 2026-09-27 07:30 UTC — pre-auth decoder sweep, userByQr gate correction, staging route-selection disclosure

Eighteen requests this cycle: sixteen GraphQL GETs (all read-only, browser UA, `Accept: application/json`, ≥2 s apart, no `Authorization` header) plus two DoH lookups. No POST, no mutation, no credential, no login, no token, no customer identifier, no voucher/ticket/order number, no PII, no DNS change, no claim attempt, no HTTP to the CNAME target. Identifiers used were the synthetic strings `"0"` and `"1"` and one reserved RFC 2606 `.invalid` address. Two of the sixteen were malformed by me (a selection set on the scalar `testing_getConfirmationCode`) and returned client-side `GRAPHQL_VALIDATION_FAILED` 400s; they touched no server state and are logged as my error, not as a finding.

**1. Pre-auth ID-decoder sweep completed, no existence oracle.** On the previously unprobed entry point `/v1/graphql`: `{order(id:"0"){__typename}}` → 200/754 B `INVALID_ID "Invalid Id for type: Order id: 0"`; `{order(id:"1")}` → 200/**754 B, byte-identical apart from the echoed id**; `{ticket(id:"1")}` → 200/747 B; `{invoice(id:"1")}` → 200/739 B; `{userById(id:"0")}` → 200/744 B. All five are `extensions.code = INVALID_ID` with `failedType` naming the entity. The `0`-versus-`1` control on `order` is the decisive part: identical status, identical length, identical code, no `data` difference — so the pre-auth surface carries no validity side channel for enumeration. This closes the id-guessing avenue for the whole four-decoder set; further guessing would be a human-with-valid-id task, not a probe task. Distinct resolver frames per type were also captured: `userById` 27843/27847, `order` 52963/52999, `ticket` 671/714, `invoice` 19772/19786, `userById`'s `Order` sibling path reaching 50114. `order` and `userById` sit in adjacent code regions, consistent with the `order.user` chaining. `invoice` returns `"data":null` (NON_NULL return type) where the others return `{"<field>":null}`.

**2. Self-correction: `userByQr` is gated; my 2026-09-26 `physical_to_profile` claim is wrong.** `{userByQr(qrCode:"0"){__typename}}` unauthenticated → 200/859 B, `extensions.code = FORBIDDEN`, `message "DEVICE_UNAUTHORIZED"`, stacktrace whose first resolver frame is `Object.userByQr (/var/task/graphql.js:40310:34)`. The gate executes before any argument handling, so a physical stub does **not** yield a digital identity. I retract the `physical_to_profile` claim: I had inferred reachability from the schema shape (`userByQr(qrCode)` → `User`) without executing it, which is exactly the class of schema-reasoning error I had already logged once. Same-cycle control on the same path: `{currentUser{__typename}}` → 200/836 B `UNAUTHENTICATED` via `Object get userId (40839:23)`. So the unauthenticated surface on `/v1/graphql` now has three request-level classes, all observed this cycle: gate-fires-first (`currentUser`, `userByQr`), decode-without-gate (`userById`, `order`, `ticket`, `invoice`), and type-layer validation (non-nullable arguments).

**3. Pre-auth role-leak payload is now precisely specified (schema, verified this cycle).** `User.privileges` is `NON_NULL > UserPrivileges` and `User.id` is `NON_NULL > ID`, so any resolved `User` returns its own id and its privilege vector. `UserPrivileges` has exactly 10 fields: `belongsToCinemaOperatingCompanies [NON_NULL!]!`, `adminForCinemas [NON_NULL!]!`, `adminForBonusPrograms [NON_NULL!]!`, then seven `Boolean!` flags — `accessRightDashboard`, `accessRightFilmStatistics`, `accessRightBonusProgram`, `accessRightCampaigns`, `adminRole`, `rootRole`, `supportRole`. The role fields are plain non-null booleans, not an enum, so a pre-auth read would yield an unconditional three-bit staff/root/support disclosure per user rather than a role name. `User` re-read at 51 fields this cycle (5822 B), including `email`, `blocked: Boolean!`, `blockedReason: UserBlockedReason`, `blockedText`, `onlineTicketingToken`, `inviteCode`, `linkedAccounts [NON_NULL!]!`, `tickets/orders/invoices/vouchers/subscriptions [NON_NULL!]!`, `birthDate`, `street`, `houseNumber`, `telephone`, `zipCode`. The `blocked`+`blockedReason` pair means such a read is also a targeted-accounts list.

**4. Write-side mirror is name-for-name on three separate write paths (schema, verified this cycle).** `CinemaOperatingCompanyData` is an `INPUT_OBJECT` with exactly six `inputFields`: `name: String`, `accessRightDashboard: Boolean`, `accessRightFilmStatistics: Boolean`, `accessRightBonusProgram: Boolean`, `accessRightCampaigning: Boolean`, `cinemasIds: [ID]`. Note the introspection lesson: for an input object the field list is `inputFields`, and `fields` is `null` — my first read returned `fields:null` and I nearly recorded a schema change; it was my wrong key. Three of the four read/write flag names are identical; the fourth differs in form (`accessRightCampaigning` inbound vs `accessRightCampaigns` outbound), which is the same right under two names and is itself the kind of asymmetry that hides a missing mapping. `updateUser(userId: ID!, ..., adminCinemaOperatingCompanyIds: [ID!], resetAppChangeBlockedUntil: Boolean)` mirrors `UserPrivileges.belongsToCinemaOperatingCompanies`/`adminForCinemas` and `User.blocked`; `increaseUserTestingStatus` mirrors `User.testingStatus`. Gates for all three are unverified and I did not execute a single mutation.

**5. Staging: unauthenticated caller controls which internal LOGIN route is invoked, and the tenant id and route are echoed back.** Same query, same second, same `.invalid` address, no `Authorization`. Production → 200/954 B, `FORBIDDEN "only available in testing environments"`, gate frame `Object.testing_getConfirmationCode (29953:60)`. Staging → 200/705 B, **no gate**, resolver ran and called out: `GET /userPasswordResets/search/findByMandatorIdAndEmailAddress?emailAddress=…&mandatorId=746241be-8a37-44d8-8690-8ad67e674a2b`, downstream `status 405`, `CINEPLEX_SERVICE_ERROR`, `service: "LOGIN"`, `data.testing_getConfirmationCode: null`. So on staging an unauthenticated caller picks the internal route via the `type` argument, and the error extensions disclose the internal route, the internal service name, and the staging tenant UUID. Three consequences, stated honestly: (a) the disclosure itself is confirmed and unauthenticated; (b) the 405 is method-not-allowed from the downstream service, so the lookup never executed and **no code oracle is demonstrated** — the earlier `CINEPLEX_SERVICE_ERROR ⇒ Reset code disclosed` inference stays unsupported, as previously recorded; (c) whether any code can be extracted depends on that method mismatch, and resolving it needs a consented staging account plus a non-destructive check. I did not change the HTTP method against the disclosed route. I also learned from the validation error that the field returns `String` — it is designed to hand back a code, which is what makes the question worth a human test rather than dismissal. `ConfirmationCodeType` is `ENUM {LOGIN_CREATION, EMAIL_CHANGE, PASSWORD_RESET}`; `PASSWORD_RESET` selected the `userPasswordResets` route, which reconciles the two internally inconsistent internal paths recorded in earlier leads (the other was the `EMAIL_CHANGE`/`userChangeEmailAddresses` route, not a contradiction).

**6. Dangling CNAME re-verified, 19th consecutive cycle.** `web-dev.cineplex.de` → DoH `Status 0`, `CNAME web.gentleglacier-dfef6458.switzerlandnorth.azurecontainerapps.io`, TTL 300. Target `A` → `Status 3` NXDOMAIN with `switzerlandnorth.azurecontainerapps.io` SOA `ns1-35.azure-dns.com / azuredns-hostmaster.microsoft.com`. Both responses carried resolver comments naming the answering servers. Still the only dangle in the 14-host sweep; still passive; claimability still unconfirmed by the provider.

**7. Automation harness is still broken, third cycle after the "DoH header fix" claim.** `probe-results.md` reached 1045 lines with 8 entries logged this cycle, every one an artifact: 403s from the urllib client, 400s from URLs with a trailing backtick, and 400s from a `dns.google` request lacking `Accept: application/dns-json`. Zero POSTs. Manual curl with a balanced, `--data-urlencode`d query returns 200 with real bodies on the same URLs. The 403/400 pattern is a tooling defect and must never be reported as server behaviour; the automated log is not usable as evidence for or against any finding.
