## STEP 7 — SUMMARY

This cycle I closed the last unauthenticated avenue on the main finding and removed one of my own false claims. The four pre-auth ID decoders all reject synthetic and guessed identifiers identically, so `order(id:"0")` and `order(id:"1")` differ only in the echoed string — the pre-auth surface has no validity side channel, and I am no longer going to guess. The IDOR case is therefore structurally complete and precisely scoped: `userById`, `order`, `ticket` and `invoice` decode before authentication while `currentUser` and `userByQr` fail closed first, and a successful read would hand back the user's own id plus seven non-null privilege booleans including `rootRole`, an `onlineTicketingToken`, and account-block status. I also caught myself asserting that a physical ticket stub would resolve to a profile; executing it showed a `DEVICE_UNAUTHORIZED` gate ahead of the argument, so that claim is retracted and recorded as such. On staging, the confirmation-code field is design-level dangerous — it returns a `String` and lets the caller pick the internal route — and it disclosed an internal LOGIN route, service name and tenant UUID to an unauthenticated caller, but the downstream 405 means I did not and could not extract a code, and I will not report one. The dangle held for a 19th cycle. Net risk remains low: 18 read-only requests, no writes, no credentials, no customer data, one self-inflicted malformed query, and three claims corrected rather than padded.
