[HYP] Pre-auth id-decode reaches the entity-lookup layer on six resolvers split across **two different decoder contracts**, with a uniform non-enumerating guardrail
class: IDOR
asset: graphql-api.app.cineplex.de — Class A: `userById`, `order`, `ticket`, `invoice`; Class B: `contentSectionList`, `movieList`; chaining `Order.user` and `Ticket.user`; mirrored byte-for-byte on graphql-api.app.staging.cineplex.de
confidence: 88
reasoning: Raised 86 → 88, and the gain is entirely on **characterization and remediation guidance**, not on impact — which remains gated on a check I cannot make unauthenticated. What is new is that the six resolvers are not one family but **two implementations**. Class A validates and raises a clean typed `IdError` on any input. Class B decodes strictly via the `base-64` module and throws on any non-alphabet byte. I proved the split rather than inferring it: `deadbeefZZ` is 10 characters, not a multiple of 4, and would fail any length-checked decoder — Class B accepted it and reached the typed lookup, returning `IdError: Id 'deadbeefZZ' not a valid MovieListId`. Meanwhile `deadbeef=` fails only on padding rules and crashes Class B, and `dead-beef` fails only on the URL-safe hyphen and crashes Class B. Length and encoding-correctness are therefore both excluded as the discriminator; the character set alone explains every observation. The reason this matters beyond tidiness: the omission is not one missing guard call, it is two guards with different contracts, and a remediation written against the visible surface (the four Class A names, all of which appear in prior reports) would leave both Class B resolvers live.
evidence_needed: ONE consented account's own `publicId`, supplied to any of the six resolvers with **no** `Authorization` header. `GET https://graphql-api.app.cineplex.de/api/graphql?query={userById(id:"<own-publicId>"){__typename}}` → HTTP 200 with data. That single request resolves the finding; there is nothing left to establish. A second consented account is required only to distinguish unauthenticated disclosure from cross-tenant disclosure.
verify_steps: Passive half complete and now mechanistically explained: `id:"0"` throws `InvalidCharacterError` at `/var/task/node_modules/base-64/base64.js:23:36` → `graphql.js:23720` before any authorization check; `id:"MQ=="` — which I generated locally from the integer 1 — passes decode and reaches the typed lookup, answering `INVALID_ID` rather than `UNAUTHENTICATED`. Staging is byte-identical (779 B / 738 B) across the same probes. Regression test for any fix, one per class: `{userById(id:"deadbeefZZ"){__typename}}` must stop returning `200`/`INVALID_ID` (Class A), and `{movieList(id:"deadbeefZZ"){__typename}}` likewise (Class B). There is no non-GraphQL relocation option — `/metrics` on this host returns the GraphQL handler error and `/a/b/c/d` executes GraphQL — so the fix must be resolver-level and must name all six.
impact: If the post-lookup ownership check is absent, unauthenticated disclosure of complete customer identity, a live bearer token for the separate ticketing system, full purchase and invoice history, and the privileged-staff population (`privileges.rootRole` is `NON_NULL`) — reachable through six resolvers and two chaining paths on an uncappable path set, where the app is a token-based public mobile client. The two-contract finding materially raises the chance of an incomplete fix. If the check is present, this degrades to documented attack surface and I will report it as such.
testability: HUMAN_ONLY

