I ran 11 read-only requests this cycle (10 prod + 1 staging, GET/OPTIONS, ≤1 rps, no auth) using balanced URL-encoded queries with a browser UA, since the harness's 403/400 entries are known artifacts. That produced two genuinely new facts and killed the last cycle's central inference.

## Step 1 — DELTA

[CHANGED] **`graphql_entrypoint_inventory` — 2 entry points → 4.**
- `/api/graphql` → `HTTP 200` `{"data":{"__typename":"Query"}}`, unauthenticated GET
- `/gql` → `HTTP 200` `{"data":{"__typename":"Query"}}`, unauthenticated GET
- Previously the harness logged both as `HTTP 400`; those were malformed-URL artifacts, not real path probes. The paths were never actually tested.
- Combined with `/` and `/graphql`, remediation scope is now four independent unauthenticated GET entry points on the prod host.

[CHANGED] **`cors_allowlist_credentialed_null` — NEW finding, high-value.**
- `Origin: null` → `200`, `access-control-allow-origin: null`, `access-control-allow-credentials: true`, `vary: Origin`
- Identical on `graphql-api.app.staging.cineplex.de` (full parity)
- First-party origins are reflected the same way: `https://cineplex.de`, `https://www.cineplex.de`, `https://app.cineplex.de` → `200` + reflected `ACAO` + `ACAC: true`
- This is explicitly **not** the already-rejected "wildcard ACAO / CORS without credentials" class. `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` is present, which is the exact precondition the prior triage dismissed.

[CHANGED] **`cors_rejection_is_a_crash` — NEW, mechanism proof.**
- `Origin: https://attacker.example` → `HTTP 500` with body `Error: Not allowed by CORS<br> &nbsp; at origin (/var/task/graphql.js:47459:17)<br> at /var/task/node_modules/cors/lib/index.js:219:13<br> at corsMiddleware (…/cors/lib/index.js:204:7)<br> at Layer.handle [as handle_request] (…/express/lib/router/layer.js:95:5)`
- Same 500 on a **nonexistent path** (`/zzz-no-such`) → the CORS middleware runs **pre-routing**, globally.
- Control (no `Origin`) → `200`. `Referer` alone, no `Origin` → `200`. So the trigger is strictly the `Origin` header.
- The `cors` library expects an origin callback to return `callback(null, false)`; this app **throws** instead. That is hand-rolled allowlist code, and it independently corroborates finding #1.
- The stack trace itself is OOS as a descriptive-error report; I am not filing it for that.

[CONFIRMED] **`cors_simple_requests_only`** — preflight `OPTIONS` with `Origin: null` requesting `POST` + custom headers returns `HTTP 403`. So non-simple requests are blocked at the edge, but simple requests (`GET`, and `POST` with `Content-Type: application/json`) require no preflight and are granted credentialed CORS.

[FALSIFIED] **`idor_ownership_omission` — DEAD, withdraw from all reports.**
- Last cycle's `contentSectionListByNameAndCinemas` `UNAUTHENTICATED` on a valid public cinema ID already disproved it. I am now formally closing it rather than carrying it as a parked High. There is no High IDOR claim against this API.

[CORRECTED] **`graphql_exposure` CVSS 7.5 is stale.** The "7.5" in the knowledge base predates the falsification and the argument/input-object reads. Honest demonstrated impact is schema/type/argument disclosure plus unauthenticated reach of the mutation surface, with **no** demonstrated data exfiltration and **no** account takeover. Marked `NEEDS-RESCORE`.

**No new assets.** 132-host baseline still current; nothing in this cycle touched breadth.

## Step 2 — FINDINGS (top 3, impact order)

1. **`cors_allowlist_credentialed_null`** — CORS allowlist with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` that includes the `null` origin, on prod and staging, with a GET-capable GraphQL surface. Status: `PROMISING`. CVSS: `TBD — pending cookie SameSite`. Evidence: `Origin: null` → 200 / `ACAO: null` / `ACAC: true` / `Vary: Origin`; three first-party origins reflected identically; staging parity; preflight 403 confirms simple-request path is open.

2. **`graphql_fourth_entrypoint`** — unauthenticated GraphQL on four paths (`/`, `/graphql`, `/api/graphql`, `/gql`). Status: `REPORTABLE (Low standalone)`. CVSS: `~5.3 (AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N)`. Evidence: 200 `{"data":{"__typename":"Query"}}` from both newly-tested paths, browser UA, balanced `--data-urlencode`. Corrects two prior false 400s.

3. **`cors_rejection_stacktrace`** — non-allowlisted `Origin` yields 500 + `/var/task` + `cors/lib` + `express` stack trace on any path. Status: `OOS (descriptive) but kept as mechanism evidence for #1`. CVSS: `N/A — do not file standalone`.

## Step 3 — HYPOTHESES (top 3, confidence order)

1. **`null_origin_cookie_ride` — 96.** The allowlisted `null` origin lets a `file://` page, `data:` URL, or any sandboxed iframe make a **credentialed** simple cross-origin GET to the GraphQL API and read the response. Impact if true: cross-origin account data read, and because simple `POST application/json` needs no preflight, cross-origin **mutations** on the victim's session — a path to account modification. `evidence_needs`: the `Set-Cookie` header from an authenticated API response, to read `SameSite`. `kill_condition`: if the session cookie is `SameSite=Lax` or `Strict` (browser default), the browser will not attach it to a cross-site credentialed XHR, impact collapses to reading the already-public anonymous data → Low.

2. **`allowlisted_first_party_xss_chain` — 80.** An XSS or user-content injection on any of `https://cineplex.de`, `https://www.cineplex.de`, or `https://app.cineplex.de` combines with credentialed CORS to read and mutate any victim's account through the API. Impact: account takeover. `evidence_needs`: a user-controlled-content surface on an allowlisted origin. `kill_condition`: if none of the three allowlisted origins serves user-generated content and none has a reflected-input injection point, the chain is dead.

3. **`cors_throw_is_global_preauth_gate` — 70.** Because the CORS middleware runs before routing, every request carrying a non-allowlisted `Origin` — including from a legitimate first-party page that sets an unexpected `Origin`, a mobile WebView, or a corporate proxy that rewrites `Origin` — fails with 500. Impact: availability/robustness regression on a customer-facing path. `evidence_needs`: a legitimate first-party client that sends a non-allowlisted `Origin`. `kill_condition`: if no first-party client ever sends a non-allowlisted `Origin`, this is inert.

## Step 4 — SELF_CRITIQUE

- **S1, likely valid:** I nearly dropped `/api/graphql` and `/gql` as "403/400 in the log, already covered" instead of testing them with a balanced query. Two real entry points were sitting in the log mislabeled as failures. This is the same harness-trust error I have now committed three times; the correct standing rule is that no `probe-results.md` status is evidence until reproduced with balanced URL encoding and a browser UA.
- **S2, likely invalid:** My CORS severity is currently unearned. I have proven the CORS *configuration*; I have not proven any data crosses a trust boundary, because I have not observed the session cookie. Calling this anything above Low right now would repeat exactly the mistake that killed the IDOR claim — inferring impact from position in the request path rather than from observed data flow. I am holding it at `TBD` on purpose.
- **S3, assumption worth challenging:** I assume a cookie-based session. The API may authenticate purely via a bearer token, in which case `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` is inert and the whole finding is decorative. Nothing in the schema or responses so far distinguishes these.
- **S4, bias:** CORS findings are the most over-reported class in bug bounty and the triager has already rejected a CORS item in this engagement. I should expect "descriptive/CORS-without-credentials" as the rejection reason and make sure the `SameSite` evidence is in hand before spending the reporter's credibility on it.
- **S5, safety:** The 500 is triggered by an ordinary client header, not an attack, and I did not attempt to induce load or repeat it in a loop. Request volume stayed at 11 for the cycle.

## Step 5 — NEXT

**[NEXT: AUTH_HELPED]** Request the `Set-Cookie` response header from a **single authenticated** `GET https://graphql-api.app.cineplex.de/graphql?query=%7B__typename%7D%7D` using a consented tester account, and read the `SameSite` attribute off the session cookie. Zero request bodies, zero field data, zero PII — one header, one request. This single observation decides hypothesis #1: `SameSite=None` promotes the CORS finding to a High-severity credentialed cross-origin finding and makes #2 a serious chain; `SameSite=Lax/Strict` collapses it to Low and I close it. This is the same blocked pattern as the `userById` test, so the reporter can service both in one sitting with one request each.

## Step 6 — LEARNING

- **A 400 or 403 in an automated log is a claim about the tool, not the target.** Both new entry points and the entire CORS surface were hidden behind malformed URLs and a urllib UA. Every "blocked" result in this engagement deserves one manual balanced reproduction before it is written down as a fact.
- **Header-triggered 500s are worth chasing precisely because they are usually OOS.** I nearly dismissed `Not allowed by CORS` as a noisy stack trace. Chasing it was what revealed the allowlist, the `null` entry, and the credentials flag. The stack trace was worthless; the control flow it exposed was the finding.
- **The falsification cascade is now complete.** IDOR is dead, the 7.5 is stale, and the only remaining unblocked question on this target is a cookie attribute. I should resist adding breadth probes here and concentrate on the one human-gated request.
- **Process defect to flag to the operator:** the automated prober scraped my previous cycle's `[NEXT] HUMAN` block and replayed it literally — `…userById(id:"<own-publicId>")…` appears in the 23:42 log. If I ever place a real identifier inside a `[NEXT]` block, the pipeline will fire it unauthenticated against live infrastructure. No real customer or account identifier may ever appear in a NEXT block; placeholders only.

## Step 7 — RISK

`2/100`. This cycle: 11 requests total (10 to `graphql-api.app.cineplex.de`, 1 to `graphql-api.app.staging.cineplex.de`), all GET or OPTIONS, all spaced ≥3 s, browser UA, no `Authorization`, no cookies sent, no request bodies, no customer or account identifiers, no PII, no writes, no state change, no DNS or Azure resource touched, no DoM attempt. The 500 was induced by a single standard `Origin` header on a single request and is not a load or availability attack.

## Step 8

- **what_did_i_find_learned:** Found two previously untested unauthenticated GraphQL entry points, `/api/graphql` and `/gql`, both `200 {"data":{"__typename":"Query"}}`, bringing the confirmed total to four and correcting two false 400s in the log. Found a credentialed CORS allowlist on both prod and staging that includes the `null` origin and reflects first-party origins with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`, and a non-allowlisted `Origin` crashing the global pre-routing CORS middleware into a `/var/task` stack trace, which is the mechanism proof that the allowlist is hand-rolled. Learned that no status in `probe-results.md` is trustworthy without a manual balanced reproduction, and that a process defect exists where the automated prober replays literal `[NEXT]` block contents against live infrastructure. Formally withdrew `idor_ownership_omission` and marked the 7.5 CVSS stale.
- **context:** Phase `POC`, target `api`. Both GraphQL hosts sit behind Cloudflare and an allowlist-performing Express/Apollo origin at `/var/task`; a plain `GET` with no query returns a native Express 400. Anthropic models were unavailable, so the deep-hunt ran on the local opencode big-pickle model. The single blocking unknown across both remaining findings is a `SameSite` cookie attribute obtainable only with one consented authenticated request.
- **contribution:** Decided the entry-point question that the harness had mis-recorded, raised the only credentialed-CORS candidate this engagement has produced, and closed out the false High so the report set stops carrying an indefensible claim.
- **success_metrics:** 4/4 entry points now confirmed rather than 2 confirmed plus 2 false failures; 1/1 planned next-step items executed (entry-point resolution, 100%); 0 unsupported severity claims outstanding; 1 falsified finding formally retired; 0 requests exceeding the rate limit; 0 out-of-scope actions.
- **learning_or_blockers:** Learned to treat every automated status as a claim about the tool rather than the target, and that the most valuable evidence in a rejected-looking 500 is the control flow it exposes. Blocked on a single human-gated item: reading `SameSite` off the session cookie, which is the sole determinant of whether the new CORS finding is High or Low, and the same request pattern already queued for the `userById` ownership test.
- **recommendation:** Service the two pending authenticated requests in one pass — the `Set-Cookie` header read for CORS severity, and the consented own-`publicId` `userById` `__typename` test for the decoder family. Independently, file `/api/graphql` and `/gql` into the GraphQL exposure report so the remediation scope is four paths, and re-score that finding down from the stale 7.5 to a figure defensible against schema disclosure plus a public-catalogue read. Stop probing this target until the human responses arrive; remaining unblocked surface is exhausted.
