[NEW] `graphql-api.app.cineplex.de/` is the ONLY POST-capable route, and it is the ONLY route that answers CORS preflight. `OPTIONS /` → **204** with `ACAO: <origin>`, `ACAC: true`, `ACAM: GET,HEAD,PUT,PATCH,POST,DELETE`, `ACAH: content-type,authorization`. Reproduced 6/6: three origins (`null`, `http://localhost:3000`, `https://app.staging.cineplex.de`) × two environments (prod, staging). All other three paths return API-GW 403 on both POST and OPTIONS.
[NEW] Preflight green-lights the exact request a third-party page would need: `POST` + `content-type: application/json` + `authorization`, credentialed, from an opaque `null` origin. The actual POST then returns 200 with `ACAO: null`, `ACAC: true` and a readable body. End-to-end at the HTTP layer, browser UA, unauthenticated.
[NEW] POST to `/graphql`, `/api/graphql`, `/gql` dies at API Gateway — `x-amzn-errortype: MissingAuthenticationTokenException`, body `{"message":"Missing Authentication Token"}`, and critically **no `x-powered-by: Express` and no `x-amz-trace-id`**. The request never reaches the Lambda. Those three are GET-only read mirrors, not transports.
[NEW] `POST /` on both envs returns 200 with `ACAO: null` + `ACAC: true` and executes GraphQL (`{"data":{"__typename":"Query"}}`). Mutations are reachable over POST on the one route that also passes preflight for hostile origins.
[CHANGED] My 2026-09-28 claim of "4 equivalent unauthenticated full-schema GraphQL surfaces", confidence 97, was wrong in a way that mattered. They ARE equivalent for read/introspection — same 83 queries, 140 mutations, 385 types, byte-identical bodies. They are NOT equivalent as transports: three of the four cannot receive a mutation. I asserted parity from identical GET bytes and never varied the method. The 403s also explain a stale inconsistency in my own log — my `/graphql` POST probes in the 2026-09-26 automated run were recorded as "zero POST captures", which was never an absence of POST capability, it was a 403 I misfiled.
[CHANGED] CORS impact ceiling moved. `ACAH: authorization` next to `ACAC: true` is the tell: a config that allows a bearer header while permitting credentials is a `credentials: true` applied without regard to the auth mechanism, not a deliberate design. That is evidence the allowlist was never reasoned about — and it is *not* evidence that cookies work. Still unresolved, still the one thing an account settles.

