[HYP] The production GraphQL API preflight-approves credentialed cross-origin POST from an opaque `null` origin, a dev loopback origin, and a staging-environment origin
class: MISCONFIG
asset: graphql-api.app.cineplex.de
confidence: 90
reasoning: Six preflights, 6/6 `204`, every one returning `access-control-allow-credentials: true`, `access-control-allow-methods: GET,HEAD,PUT,PATCH,POST,DELETE`, `access-control-allow-headers: content-type,authorization`, with `ACAO` echoing the attacker's exact origin — across `null`, `http://localhost:3000`, `https://app.staging.cineplex.de` on both prod and staging. The non-preflight `POST /` returns 200 `{"data":{"__typename":"Query"}}` with `ACAO: null`, `ACAC: true`. CORS headers are unconditional across auth state: an unauthenticated `__typename` and a gate-rejected `currentUser` (200 + `UNAUTHENTICATED` body, `graphql.js:40839`) both carry the same ACAO/ACAC pair, so the policy runs before resolver authorization. A disallowed origin is thrown at `at origin (/var/task/graphql.js:47459:17)` as HTTP 500 — one global pre-routing middleware, one allowlist, all routes.
evidence_needed: whether the API honours a cookie-derived session. This is the single fact that separates the two impact tiers below. `currentUser{id}` is byte-identical with and without `Cookie: cineplex_session=AAAA; JSESSIONID=BBBB; jwt=CCCC`, and no `Set-Cookie` was seen on any route × env — so bearer-only is fully consistent with everything measured.
verify_steps: completed — `curl -sS --http2 -X OPTIONS -H "Origin: null" -H "Access-Control-Request-Method: POST" -H "Access-Control-Request-Headers: content-type,authorization" "https://graphql-api.app.cineplex.de/"`; then POST `-d '{"query":"{__typename}"}'` with `Content-Type: application/json` and `Origin: null`; repeat with `-H "Origin: http://localhost:3000"`.
impact TIER 1 (no account, proven now): any page served with `Content-Security-Policy: sandbox` has an opaque `null` origin, and from it a `fetch(..., {credentials:"include"})` passes preflight and reads the response. The attacker needs no infrastructure of their own. On staging that includes invoking `testing_getConfirmationCode`, which resolves with no environment guard and has persisted 11+ cycles — so a visitor to an attacker's page can be made to fire that server-side. That is an abuse primitive over 140 mutations, not just a catalogue read. I did not run it: POST to it is a mutating test and the program forbids those, and I am not sending a mutation document to find out. That boundary holds. I also have not run this inside a real browser with a real sandboxed null-origin document — the preflight and POST responses are directly measured, the browser's decision to honor them is spec behaviour I am asserting, not observing.
impact TIER 2 (needs AUTH_HELPED): the same primitive with cookies riding, against a host serving a 51-field `User` (email, birthDate, `onlineTicketingToken`, `inviteCode`, `blocked`, full order and invoice history) and 15 resolvers that decode IDs before their auth gate. That is the difference between Medium and High/Critical. I am not claiming Tier 2 until an account proves a cookie session exists.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] Staging carries the identical hostile-origin credentialed POST grant, over the one environment that hosts an unauthenticated mutation
class: MISCONFIG
asset: graphql-api.app.staging.cineplex.de
confidence: 78
reasoning: Identical preflight behaviour to prod on all three hostile origins (3/3 `204`, `ACAC: true`, POST + `authorization` allowed), and `POST /` executes GraphQL with `ACAO: null` + `ACAC: true`. The staging-specific fact is that the same route can also reach `testing_getConfirmationCode`, which has no environment guard — so the staging grant is not a copy of prod's risk, it is the one place where the grant lands on an operation that needs no credential. `testing_getConfirmationCode` currently returns a method-mismatch error, which limits this to a method-acceptance question I am not resolving by sending a mutation.
evidence_needed: that `testing_getConfirmationCode` is executable rather than permanently method-blocked. Requires a consented account and an authorized tester — the confirmation-code flow sends a real OTP to a real user, so I will not attempt it or trigger it against a stranger's identifier.
verify_steps: preflight and POST parity confirmed same-cycle. The remaining step is account-gated and human-only.
impact: Staging is the environment where a CORS fix scoped to production leaves the equivalent grant fully open AND reachable to an unauthenticated mutation. This is a scoping finding for remediation as much as a vulnerability: fixing prod's allowlist alone closes neither staging nor the shared middleware. Medium now, inherits Tier 2 if a cookie session exists.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

