## Report: Cineplex — GraphQL API credentialed-CORS misconfiguration, unauthenticated introspection, and dangling CNAME

**Target:** `graphql-api.app.cineplex.de` (prod), `graphql-api.app.staging.cineplex.de`, `web-dev.cineplex.de`
**Date:** 2026-09-29 · **Testing:** unauthenticated, passive-first, no customer data touched, no mutations executed

---

### 1. Credentialed CORS with a hostile origin allowlist — Medium (5.4)

`AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N`

The production GraphQL API reflects the request `Origin` and sets `access-control-allow-credentials: true` for three origins, on every GraphQL route and in both environments:

```
$ curl -sS -X OPTIONS "https://graphql-api.app.cineplex.de/" \
  -H "Origin: null" -H "Access-Control-Request-Method: POST" \
  -H "Access-Control-Request-Headers: content-type,authorization" -D - -o /dev/null

HTTP/2 204
access-control-allow-origin: null
access-control-allow-credentials: true
access-control-allow-methods: GET,HEAD,PUT,PATCH,POST,DELETE
access-control-allow-headers: content-type,authorization
vary: Origin, Access-Control-Request-Headers
```

Trusted origins, 6/6 across both environments: `null`, `http://localhost:3000`, `https://app.staging.cineplex.de`. Any other origin gets `500` (`Error: Not allowed by CORS`, frame `/var/task/graphql.js:47459:17`) — so this is a fixed allowlist, not a blanket `*`.

**Cross-origin read is confirmed on GET** (no POST needed):

```
$ curl -sS "https://graphql-api.app.cineplex.de/?query=%7B__typename%7D" -H "Origin: null" -D -
HTTP/2 200
access-control-allow-origin: null
access-control-allow-credentials: true
{"data":{"__typename":"Query"}}
```

`ACAC: true` on a response means a browser hands that body to attacker JavaScript.

**Stated honestly — what I could not prove.** I could not demonstrate account compromise, and I want to be explicit about why rather than let the header pair imply it:

- `login`/`socialLogin`/`refreshLogin` return `LoginReturnType{user, posApp, fileServerToken, jwt, csrf, roles, refreshToken}`. The credential arrives **in the response body as a bearer token**, the signature of a client that holds it in JS storage rather than an ambient cookie.
- A `GET` carrying `Cookie: session=…; connect.sid=…; JSESSIONID=…; jwt=…; auth=1` returned a body **byte-identical** to the no-cookie baseline (both md5 `c2b4dd586b0b294977f7def837302227`).
- I could not read the presumed first-party client's storage mechanism: `app.cineplex.de` returns `403` with `cf-mitigated: challenge`.

So as verified, a cross-origin attacker page cannot obtain the victim's bearer token, and this grants no access beyond what an unauthenticated attacker already has. I am scoring the **misconfiguration**, not a compromise. It becomes critical the moment any first-party web client authenticates to this API by cookie — and `storeSDKLogin(data: SDKLoginInput{cookieId, datetime, userId})` shows a cookie credential exists somewhere in this system. **A test account would settle this in one request**, and I would ask the program to run it if it maintains one.

### 2. Unauthenticated full-schema GraphQL introspection — Medium (5.3)

`AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N`

Introspection is open with no auth on prod and staging, exposing **385 types, 83 active Query fields, 140 active Mutation fields**, with no environment partitioning. All four routes `/`, `/graphql`, `/api/graphql`, `/gql` return byte-identical schema over GET.

Route caveat, verified by method variation: only `/` accepts POST. The other three are **GET-only read mirrors** — `POST`/`OPTIONS` return API Gateway `403` `MissingAuthenticationTokenException`.

Relevant to authorization testing: the schema declares no auth directives at all (only `@cost`, `@costFactor`, `@skip`, `@include`, `@deprecated`), so authorization is enforced purely at resolver runtime and no mutation can be triaged as public or gated by reading the schema.

### 3. Dangling CNAME on `web-dev.cineplex.de` — Low, unproven claimability

18th consecutive cycle, re-verified with correctly-formed DoH:

```
CNAME web-dev.cineplex.de → web.gentleglacier-dfef6458.switzerlandnorth.azurecontainerapps.io   Status 0
A     web.gentleglacier-dfef6458.switzerlandnorth.azurecontainerapps.io                       Status 3 (NXDOMAIN)
      SOA switzerlandnorth.azurecontainerapps.io → azure-dns.com
```

The `gentleglacier` environment appears in exactly one inventory record, so a successful claim yields one hostname. I have **not** claimed the environment, so I am not asserting takeover — an Azure Container Apps environment name must be reusable to make this exploitable, which is provider-side and needs confirmation from a Cineplex Azure account.

---

### Withdrawn

I had `contentSectionListByNameAndCinemas` flagged as a High IDOR across ~27 cycles based on invalid IDs returning `INVALID_ID` while sibling controls returned `UNAUTHENTICATED`. I falsified my own finding: a **valid public cinema ID** also returned `UNAUTHENTICATED` at `selectedCinemaIdsDb`. The `INVALID_ID` signature measures *decode position*, not *check presence*. I am withdrawing it rather than re-scoring it.

**Strongest untested lead (account-gated):** `storeSDKLogin(data: SDKLoginInput{cookieId, datetime, userId}) -> {success: Boolean}` takes a client-supplied `userId` and `cookieId` — the shape of a session-binding endpoint that may run before its auth check. It is a mutation, so I did not execute it.

**[NEXT] PROBE:** with a consented Cineplex test account, serve a `Content-Security-Policy: sandbox` page that issues `fetch("https://graphql-api.app.cineplex.de/", {method:"POST", credentials:"include", headers:{"content-type":"application/json"}, body:'{"query":"{currentUser{id}}"}'})` — a non-null `currentUser` closes finding 1 at High, an `UNAUTHENTICATED` result closes it at Low; then call `storeSDKLogin` with a self-owned `userId` to test for pre-auth session minting.
