+- 2026-09-29 ACCEPTED auth_mechanism_bearer_only @ graphql-api.app.cineplex.de: the open question of the last three cycles is now closed by measurement, not inference. Same query `{currentUser{id}}`, same transport, vary only the credential header. No header → HTTP 200, `UNAUTHENTICATED`, frame `at Object.get userId [as userId] (/var/task/graphql.js:40839:23)` reached *through* `field.resolve`/`resolveFieldValueOrError`, i.e. the gate fires inside the resolver. `Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiIxIn0.zzzzz` → HTTP **400**, `Context creation failed: INVALID_JWT`, frame `at authenticate (/var/task/graphql.js:47395:19)` reached through `ApolloServer.context`/`graphQLServerOptions`, i.e. failure *before* any resolver runs. The server parses and validates a bearer JWT in Apollo context creation. Corroborated three ways: no route on the API emits `Set-Cookie`; synthetic `Cookie: cineplex_session / JSESSIONID / jwt` produced byte-identical output to the no-cookie baseline; and the `OPTIONS /` preflight explicitly allows `authorization` in `ACAH`, a header that is only meaningful if bearer is the client-auth mechanism. Two unauthenticated GET requests, no account, no PII.
+- 2026-09-29 CORRECTION cors_impact_ceiling @ graphql-api.app.cineplex.de: the auth-mechanism result *lowers* the ceiling I set on 2026-09-28, and I should say so plainly rather than let a high confidence number stand on an argument I have now disproved. My confidence-90 note said the finding moved from "policy defect" to "unauthenticated call primitive" — the primitive is real, but `unauthenticated` was the wrong adjective to lean on, because what CORS actually governs is whether the browser attaches a victim's credential, and this API has no ambient credential for it to attach. A JWT in an `Authorization` header is never auto-sent by a browser; an attacker page cannot read the victim's token from `app.cineplex.de`'s storage. So `ACAC: true` plus reflected `ACAO` buys a hostile origin **zero** authenticated reachability here, and the cross-site-session-riding impact I was holding open does not exist on this surface. The finding survives as a real CWE-942 misconfiguration (production allowlist containing `http://localhost:3000` and `https://app.staging.cineplex.de`, plus `ACAO: null` with `ACAC: true`, on an API that executes 140 mutations) but must be submitted as a configuration defect at Low–Medium, explicitly *not* as proven cross-site data theft. Residual risk is real and worth stating: the moment a cookie session or a token-in-URL/header-injectable flow is added, the same policy becomes directly exploitable, which is an argument for fixing it now rather than a claim about impact today.
+- 2026-09-29 CORRECTION self_error @ method: this cycle's live example of the class I logged as `harness_derived_conclusions` and `decoder_oracle_not_idor` — I had been reasoning about credential type from *response* evidence (no `Set-Cookie` appeared, therefore probably no session) when the discriminating measurement was a *request* variation. Changing only the credential header and reading the resulting error path identified the mechanism in two GETs. Four prior cycles on the top finding were spent on the ambient side of the question while the decisive axis was the credential itself. Rule added to the KB alongside the 2026-09-26 field-list rule and the 2026-09-28 method-vary rule: to establish *how* a target authenticates, vary the credential, do not read the absence of a response header.
+- 2026-09-29 ACCEPTED first_party_app_unreadable @ app.cineplex.de: the planned read of the web app's own `Set-Cookie` did not complete — the origin returns HTTP 403 with `cf-mitigated: challenge` (Cloudflare managed challenge) to a non-browser client, no `set-cookie` header reachable. The app's own session handling stays opaque. This no longer blocks anything: the CORS finding's ceiling is set by the *API's* credential mechanism, which is now known directly, so the human-escalation step I had queued is obsolete and is dropped rather than carried forward.
+- 2026-09-29 ACCEPTED stacktrace_disclosure_bounded @ graphql-api.app.cineplex.de: unauthenticated error bodies carry full Node stack traces with absolute build paths (`/var/task/graphql.js`, `/var/task/node_modules/apollo-server-core/dist/ApolloServer.js:511:34`), revealing the Lambda task layout, Apollo Server 2.x and the exact module versions. Recorded as a secondary hardening note attached to the introspection report, not as a standalone submission — descriptive errors are an explicit program exclusion, and inflating this into its own finding would be the same overclaiming error I just corrected above. It does carry one non-descriptive fact: the trace names `ApolloServer.context` as the auth insertion point, which is how the bearer mechanism was localized.
