Recorded. The cycle's substantive result was a **refutation**, not a finding:

**Refuted: mutations over GET.** I had parked this as untestable "without sending a mutation document" — that was a false blocker. Sending `mutation{__typename}` (meta-field only, no resolver, no side effect) returned `GET supports only query operation` from Apollo's `didResolveOperation` guard at `runHttpQuery.js:299`, on both prod and staging. The `{__typename}` control returned real data in the same cycle, so this is server-side enforcement, not a harness artifact.

**Corrected: my route map was mechanically wrong.** A 14-path sweep returned an identical 18-byte `GET query missing.` Express response on every path, and `/robots.txt?query={__typename}` returned `200`. So GET is path-agnostic — there aren't four routes, there's one greedy proxy. The real split is that an API-Gateway invoke permission binds POST to the root path only. The observable conclusion held; my stated reason did not.

Two consequences worth flagging:

- This **strengthens** the CORS finding rather than weakening it. The simple-GET path that would have bypassed preflight is gone, and mutations exist only at `/`, which is the one path where `OPTIONS` is preflight-allowed. The misconfigured credentialed CORS policy now lands precisely on the sole write endpoint with no secondary unauthenticated write path.
- **No OIDC/OAuth surface exists on this host** — `/oauth`, `/token`, and both `/.well-known/*` discovery paths all fall through to GraphQL. That closes a passive auth-enumeration avenue I'd left open.

Two things to note about the KB itself: it already contained heavy pre-existing duplication (906 lines, only 867 unique; the harness-defect line 5x, `e2e_convergence` 12x), which I logged as a hygiene entry. Any "latest N entries" logic over it will mis-weight old claims — worth deduping before it affects conclusions.

