I stopped short of a `sync-issues.yml` run — nothing is committed, and the diffs are reviewable.

## What I found

Three claims in the prior "Completed" section were **false on disk**:

1. **`CANONICAL STATUS` was never written.** `grep -i canonical knowledge/index.md` → zero hits. The 2026-09-30 entry asserting it existed (and that `sync-issues.py` "cannot break" on it) was fiction.
2. **The 703 `[HYP]`→`[LEARN]` retags were never applied.** 1131 `[HYP]` blocks were still publishable, 298 IDOR-family — including titles asserting *"GET-Verified Cross-Tenant PII"* and *"Cross-Tenant PII Dump"* on a claim that is falsified *and* where no customer record was ever touched.
3. **`publish_path_clear` ("only three live `[HYP]` blocks")** asserted a system property after inspecting one lead file — the exact part→system error the KB's own last line warns about.

The falsification was also *inverted*: the newest KB line read `ACCEPTED idor_booking … corroborating the omission`, re-asserting what the 2026-09-30 entry marked DEAD.

## What I changed

- **Retagged 260 falsified IDOR blocks → `[LEARN]`** (`[falsified: authz-omission]` prefix), tag-level per the KB's own prescription that append-only corrections lose to the claims they correct. Also 2 mis-tagged non-findings, 3 unfilled `[HYP] <title>` template scaffolds, and 5 orphaned `class: 'MISCONFIG' conf: '93'` merge lines.
- **Created `CANONICAL STATUS` at the top** with an explicit precedence rule ("recency is not authority"), separating LIVE / DEAD / SURVIVING / METHOD RULES. I deliberately did *not* rewrite the ~90 stale `ACCEPTED idor_booking` log lines — that would destroy the audit trail; the precedence rule handles them instead.
- **Root-caused and fixed the harness defect** in `.github/workflows/hunt.yml`: the regex `https?://[^\s"\)\]\}]+` excluded `)]}` but not a backtick, so `` https://app.staging.cineplex.de` `` was probed as a hostname. Diagnosed 2026-09-30, still emitting at 2026-10-01 19:18.

Verification used `sync-issues.py`'s own `parse_blocks`/`fingerprint` (my first replication was wrong — I'd omitted `HYP` from `STOP` and missed `split_inline`). Real result: **866 blocks, 0 falsified-IDOR leaks**, 1131 → 866 as expected.

## One correction and one caveat

My earlier "3 invalid class / missing asset" read was **my own replication bug**, not a file defect — `split_inline` legitimately handles those one-line forms. I re-verified against the real module.

Remaining P1, which I did **not** act on: 176/866 blocks have prose-polluted `asset` fields driving 59 fingerprints from 866 blocks. But `asset` *is* the fingerprint key and some values are legitimately multi-valued (`graphql-api.app.{,staging.}cineplex.de`, `…{/,/graphql}`). Blanket truncation to the leading host would corrupt identity, so it needs per-block review — worth flagging before any first `sync-issues.yml` run opens duplicate issues.
