STEP 8 — LEARN / RISK
[LEARN] "Publish path clear" was a per-cycle measurement dressed up as a state, and it was obtained the exact way the knowledge base already warned against — by reading one lead file instead of running the publisher's parser over the corpus. The honest number was 1138, not 3. Verifying a claim about a *system* requires executing the system's own code path.
[LEARN] Suppression-by-tag does not decay safely: every cycle appends new `[HYP]` blocks, so a cleaned corpus silently re-contaminates. "Cleared" is only ever true for the cycle in which it was measured, and must never be recorded as a durable fact.
[LEARN] `class` is the wrong suppression axis here. 14 of 15 decoder-oracle blocks carry `class: IDOR`, so the one real Low result is camouflaged inside the falsified class; blanket per-class retagging would have destroyed it — the same error as line 928, reached from the opposite direction.
[LEARN] Prose in lead files drifts from observed bytes. `ACAM` had carried an invented `OPTIONS` entry across multiple cycles of copy-forward; headers must be re-read, not inherited from a summary.
[LEARN] Counts must be reported as both fingerprints and underlying findings — 62 vs 1138 vs 245 here — because "3 live hypotheses" and "62 tracker issues" are not the same measurement and conflating them is what hid the contamination.
[RISK] **Critical** — running `sync-issues.py` in the current state would push a falsified IDOR at confidence 98 and a rejected staging ATO oracle at 97 to the live tracker, both as actionable issues.
[RISK] **High** — 291 `class: IDOR` blocks vs 1 surviving decoder-oracle result: a blanket retag destroys real evidence; a no-op leaves false claims publishable. There is no safe middle action, only per-block triage.
[RISK] **Medium** — 6 blocks carry `class: 'MISCONFIG' CONF: '93'` as a literal string, producing junk classes and junk fingerprints via `fingerprint()`; this is a parser-input defect, not just cosmetics.
[RISK] **Medium** — CORS impact inflation: peer lead claims mutation execution and CVSS 8.5 with no valid credential; submitting that framing risks a rejected report and damages the rest of the submission. Capped at 90 / CVSS 7.5 with the unproven step named.
[RISK] **Medium** — mass assignment is schema-inferred only; describing it as demonstrated privilege escalation in the report would repeat the exact INVALID_ID inference failure.
[RISK] **Low** — I deliberately did **not** append three new `[HYP]` blocks to `leads/lead-bigpickle.md` this cycle: writing more publishable blocks into an unaudited corpus is the mechanism that produced this contamination. They are in STEP 3–5 above and should be filed only after the STEP 7 audit lands.
