- 2026-10-03 A retraction is a **claim-level** record (`retractions.json`), not a class filter and not a file edit. `retraction_for()` matches on fingerprint or title substrings with an explicit `title_excludes` spare-list, and the publisher honours it in both directions: retracted blocks are never created, and retracted published issues are closed as `false-positive` with the falsifying evidence quoted. It is additive, reviewable, and reversible — every entry must name the test that falsified it.
- 2026-10-03 The spare-list is load-bearing, not decoration. 8 decoder-oracle blocks carry `class: IDOR` on the same asset as the 267 retracted ones; a title match alone would have suppressed the single Low finding that survived the falsification. Verified by execution: all 8 remain live, and 267 blocks / 4 fingerprints retract.
- 2026-10-03 `fingerprint()` is `asset+class` only, so a falsified claim and the real finding that replaced it collide. Unpatched, dedup closed the survivor as a duplicate **of the false issue**. `pick_keeper()` now refuses a retracted issue as group survivor; regression-tested through the full publish path against a stubbed tracker.
- 2026-10-03 `python3 scripts/sync-issues.py --audit` is the standing verification command and prints both fingerprints and underlying blocks. The KB line "verifying a claim about a system requires executing the system's own code path" is now a command, not an aspiration. Claiming the publish path is clear without running it has been wrong in three consecutive cycles; treat any future "publish path clear" claim without this command's output as unverified.
