Both planned actions are done, and step 2 surfaced a bug that invalidated a prior claim.

## 1. Verifier defects fixed (`.github/workflows/hunt.yml`)

| Line | Defect | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| 210 | regex char class lacked `` ` `` | added `` ` `` |
| 216 | `strip()` set lacked `` ` `` | added `` ` `` |
| 222 | `urls[:12]` cap | `urls[:60]` |
| 225 | `Mozilla/5.0 (passive verifier)` bot UA | full Chrome 120 UA |

Validated on the exact strings that produced the old failures: **3 backtick-contaminated URLs eliminated, all legitimate URLs preserved.**

## 2. Live re-verification (read-only, 1 rps)

- **DoH CNAME** — `Status0`, `web-dev.cineplex.de` → `web.gentleglacier-dfef6458.switzerlandnorth.azurecontainerapps.io.`, TTL 300. No backtick 400, no bot 403.
- **GraphQL arbitrary unshadowed path** — balanced `query=%7B__typename%7D` → `200 {"data":{"__typename":"Query"}}`.
- **CORS preflight re-confirmed same-cycle** on `graphql-api.app.cineplex.de/`, `Origin: https://app.staging.cineplex.de`:
  - `access-control-allow-origin: https://app.staging.cineplex.de` (staging origin trusted by **production**)
  - `access-control-allow-credentials: true`
  - `access-control-allow-headers: content-type,authorization`
  - `access-control-allow-methods: GET,HEAD,PUT,PATCH,POST,DELETE` (no `OPTIONS`)
  - `vary: Origin, Access-Control-Request-Headers`
  - `OPTIONS` on unshadowed paths → `403`; root `/` is the only POST-capable preflight surface.

Two of my own probes were initially wrong and I corrected them: I double-encoded the query param (GraphQL `400 GRAPHQL_PARSE_FAILED` was mine, not the target's), and my CORS header lookup was case-sensitive while the server sends lowercase headers.

## 3. Correction: `publish_path_clear` is falsified

Running the parser against the real lead files:

- **1221** `[HYP]` blocks parse; **301 carry `class: IDOR`** — the prior claim that IDOR text lives only in `[LEARN]` lines was false.
- Blast radius is bounded by `fingerprint()` = `md5(asset|class)` plus in-run dedup: **70** unique fingerprints, **10** of them the falsified IDOR family.
- Worse, `fingerprint()` ignores the title when an asset exists, so **115 blocks collide on `ed36cb0858b4`** — the falsified "IDOR/BOLA on user/order/ticket/voucher" title appears first and wins the slot.
- `rejected` is built *after* creation and only closes existing issues; it never guards `create`. So a workflow run would republish retracted findings.

## 4. Fix applied (`scripts/sync-issues.py` + new `retracted-findings.txt`)

Added a pre-dedup retraction gate: `load_retracted()` reads an auditable, append-only class list, and retracted blocks are dropped *before* fingerprinting — so they can neither publish nor occupy a slot that would suppress a distinct surviving hypothesis.

Verified: **1221 → 920 blocks, 301 IDOR dropped, 70 → 60 publishable fingerprints, zero IDOR remaining**, and all three canonical survivors (CORS, mass assignment, dangling CNAME) intact.

`py_compile` and YAML parse both pass. Changes are uncommitted; `analyst-*.txt`/`ctx.txt` remain untracked and untouched.

## Open decision for you

I deliberately did **not** change `fingerprint()` to include a title component. It would fix the general collapse of distinct claims sharing one `(asset, class)` slot — visible in many non-IDOR families too — but it changes dedup granularity and issue volume across the whole tracker. Existing issues are safe (their fp is pinned in the `<!-- fingerprint:... -->` comment), but new runs would fan out substantially. Want me to make the fingerprint title-aware, or leave dedup as-is and rely on the retraction gate?
