Done. Both recorded work-stream items are closed, and executing them surfaced substantially more than the summary claimed.

## Probe harness (`hunt.yml`) — 7 defects, not 3

The three recorded defects were the least important. **Root cause: all three source globs matched zero files** (`art/lead_*.txt`, `lead_*.txt`, `analyst-*.out` don't exist here), so the verifier read nothing and printed `no probes (allow=)` every cycle for ~20 cycles. The log wasn't degraded — it was absent, which voids every automated-log-derived inference, not just its noise.

Also found while fixing:
- **`}` excluded from the URL charset** truncated every GraphQL verification URL to `?query={__typename` — malformed, would 400 and be misread as "endpoint dead".
- **No scope filter** meant CORS test origins (`evil.example`, `localhost:3000`) and third parties (`dns.google`, `img.youtube.com`) were probed as program assets.
- `urls[:12]` probed 12 of 621 candidates (2%).

Now: 414 in-scope candidates / 67 hosts, 192 out-of-scope skipped, placeholders rejected, round-robin so the first 67 probes cover every host, explicit `PROBE_MAX` with counts logged (truncation can't be silent), and `SystemExit` if globs match nothing.

## Publisher (`sync-issues.py`) — the retraction loop didn't exist

Measuring from the real parser rather than one lead file: **1224 `[HYP]` blocks → 70 distinct fingerprints, 10 of which were IDOR-class** — the falsified systemic IDOR was being republished every cycle. The earlier "IDOR clean, 297→0" claim was wrong.

- Added `knowledge/suppressed.txt` (fingerprints + written retraction reasons), honored every run: 70 → 59, dropping all 11 retracted findings.
- `ensure_label()` returned `None` in both branches (pre-existing) — latent until retraction code needed it for `add_to_labels`.
- Fingerprint is `md5(asset|class)`, so one dangling-CNAME root became 7 issues; added near-duplicate collapse that unions assets so no host is lost. 59 → 57.
- Verified offline against a mock GitHub: idempotent across runs, `CLOSE_SUPPRESSED=1` closes + labels retractions, default is dry-run.

Two things I deliberately did **not** force: ~5 dangle variants carry divergent class labels (`MISCONFIG`/`OTHER`/`SUBDOMAIN_TAKEOVER`) and merge would destroy taxonomy signal, and suppression is per-fingerprint, never per-class — IDOR is the program-prioritized class, so blanket suppression would kill the next real IDOR.

No live probes were run and nothing was committed. Survivors unchanged (CORS 88, mass assignment 85, dangle 72), all still gated on human/provider action.
